00:29:13.594 --> 00:29:15.760
One example of that we're
going to walk through here

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:16.800
is with Food Lion.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.840
And Food Lion's a grocery store
chain within the southeast

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:21.850
United States.

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:24.400
And with that, as we've
talked about today with data,

00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:26.920
we run into lots of issues
of lots of individuals

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:28.220
are working with data.

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:31.330
There's lots of systems
that that data is housed in,

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:33.911
and that creates a
lot of complications.

00:29:33.911 --> 00:29:35.410
And then with Food
Lion, we actually

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:37.840
run into even bigger
complications in the fact

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:40.480
that we're not just dealing
with individuals and systems,

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:42.771
but we're also dealing with
lots of different agencies,

00:29:42.771 --> 00:29:44.260
organizations, and vendors.

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:46.510
And so I want to talk
through how we've actually

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:49.660
sought to simplify a lot of that
using the solutions of Google

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:53.110
Sheets as well as Data Studio.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.790
So as we started
working with Food Lion,

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:56.248
there were a few
things that we ran

00:29:56.248 --> 00:29:58.300
into that we knew we
had areas of disconnect

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:00.100
in how we worked
with their data.

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:02.590
The first area is
with social data.

00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:05.207
With social data,
with Food Lion,

00:30:05.207 --> 00:30:06.790
like many different
companies, they're

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:09.270
on many different
social platforms.

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:11.110
And so that was the
first thing that we had.

00:30:11.110 --> 00:30:13.840
We had different data sets,
different user interfaces

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:15.310
for their social data.

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:17.800
On top of that, they used
different organizations

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:20.710
and different vendors
for their CPC campaigns

00:30:20.710 --> 00:30:23.230
as well as on top of that for
their social communication

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:24.184
in general.

00:30:24.184 --> 00:30:25.600
The next area that
I'll talk about

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.880
is going to be in-store
coupon activity.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:30.160
This is a third party
vendor that they

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.170
use for customers
that need to activate

00:30:32.170 --> 00:30:34.390
coupons for their
loyalty card as well

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:35.742
as redeem them in-store.

00:30:35.742 --> 00:30:38.200
And all of that data is actually
hosted in that third party

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:41.134
vendor system with
limited access.

00:30:41.134 --> 00:30:43.300
And then finally, I'm going
to talk about connecting

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:47.480
online to in-store behavior.

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:50.630
And what that really is that
Food Lion has a website that

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:52.531
doesn't actually sell anything.

00:30:52.531 --> 00:30:54.530
And so what really matters
at the end of the day

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:57.290
are are their customers
actually going to in-store

00:30:57.290 --> 00:30:59.910
and how does the website
activity influence that.

00:30:59.910 --> 00:31:04.290
So we needed to join data
from multiple systems.

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:06.720
So touching the first
one of social data.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:08.780
Really what we sought
to do here was actually

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:11.210
moving all of their data
from multiple systems

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:12.500
into one system.

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:14.540
So you see here that
their primary areas

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:17.864
are looking at Facebook,
Twitter, and Pinterest.

00:31:17.864 --> 00:31:20.030
And so with that there's a
lot of different datasets

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:21.405
and a lot of
different interfaces

00:31:21.405 --> 00:31:22.490
that we're working with.

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:25.500
And so we were able to utilize
the previously mentioned

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.640
add-on Supermetrics in order
to take the powerful feature

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:32.390
that Sheets has and plug-in an
add-on to connect to the APIs

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:35.510
for Facebook, the APIs for
Twitter, as well as Pinterest.

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:38.000
And from that we were able to
actually put all of that data

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:41.600
into an automatically updated
sheet within Google Sheets

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.970
to have all raw
data into one place.

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:45.500
So now all of
these teams weren't

00:31:45.500 --> 00:31:48.110
having to actually go
get the original data

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:50.510
from each of those interfaces.

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:54.110
And then beyond that we then
were able to clean it up,

00:31:54.110 --> 00:31:55.970
put it into a schema
that Data Studio

00:31:55.970 --> 00:31:58.100
could have read as a
data source in order

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:01.430
to have beautiful visual
reports that actually matched

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:02.930
the branding for
Food Lion instead

00:32:02.930 --> 00:32:07.910
of just using the interface
of, say, Facebook Insights.

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:10.230
And so how we were
able to do that is we

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:11.910
were able to move
just from having

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:14.250
the sheets and the raw
data, which is super helpful

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:17.280
to have in one place,
but move from that

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:19.830
by having the powerful
features of the formulas

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:22.420
that exist inside of Sheets.

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:25.110
And so as you see
there on the left,

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:27.540
we had a very complex formula
that would automatically

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:31.050
update based on what day
it was to always look back

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:34.147
into a clean dataset looking
at last month's social data.

00:32:34.147 --> 00:32:35.730
And from this, we
were able to combine

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:37.410
a lot of datasets
that actually weren't

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:41.042
possible within the
Facebook Insights interface.

00:32:41.042 --> 00:32:43.500
And so here we have the example
of how we were able to show

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:47.530
them the Facebook post
engagement-- likes, comments,

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:49.350
and shares that
were taking place--

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:52.350
broken out by day of week
as well as by post type.

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:55.380
And so what this allowed was
actually a much faster way

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:58.334
to get insights for their data
that actually led to action.

00:32:58.334 --> 00:33:00.000
They were able to
take that information,

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.100
know what day of week
their audience was

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:04.560
active on Facebook, as
well as what type of posts

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:06.518
would actually get the
most amount of response.

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:12.099
The next area is going to be the
in-store coupon activity area.

00:33:12.099 --> 00:33:14.390
So the way that this works
since this was a third party

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:18.740
vendor working with their data
and just sending it back out

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:21.230
because there was limited
access to that raw data,

00:33:21.230 --> 00:33:25.470
they were actually just emailing
a spreadsheet file every month

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:27.346
to the main point of
contact at Food Lion.

00:33:27.346 --> 00:33:28.970
And then from that
throughout the month

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:31.040
as people needed
access to that data

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.160
for coupon activations
and redemptions,

00:33:34.160 --> 00:33:36.200
they were actually
just emailing it

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:38.210
to the team
throughout the month.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:39.614
This creates a
lot of complexity,

00:33:39.614 --> 00:33:41.780
and a lot of problems that
can come about from this.

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:43.880
Because by the end of
the month, you might even

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:45.530
have adjustments that
were made to the file

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:47.530
and people are looking
at different sets of data

00:33:47.530 --> 00:33:49.700
as a result. So what
we were able to do

00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:52.760
was to work with that
vendor along with Food Lion

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:55.937
and use Google Sheets
as a central point.

00:33:55.937 --> 00:33:57.770
So no longer we're
having the inefficiencies

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:00.320
of emailing files
around in order

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:03.069
to get that type of
information collaboratively.

00:34:03.069 --> 00:34:04.610
And then from there,
we were actually

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:06.620
able to take them
from people having

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:08.750
different versions
of a spreadsheet file

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:11.000
that had been emailed
to them, and actually

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:13.250
put it into Data
Studio in order to have

00:34:13.250 --> 00:34:15.050
now interactive reports.

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:18.150
Instead of just having
a flat spreadsheet that

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:20.332
has multiple different
forms throughout the month,

00:34:20.332 --> 00:34:21.790
we were actually
able to show this.

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:24.373
And here's the example we used
of a scatterplot inside of Data

00:34:24.373 --> 00:34:26.989
Studio in order to show
the relationship of coupon

00:34:26.989 --> 00:34:30.260
redemptions compared
to coupon activations.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:33.980
So a shopper puts a coupon
onto their loyalty card,

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.350
and does it actually result
for that product category,

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:38.221
does it result in an
in-store purchase?

00:34:38.221 --> 00:34:40.429
Because at the end of the
day, what they need to know

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:42.710
is what type of
product categories

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:44.900
are actually driving
users to not just be

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:46.969
interested in having the
coupon on their card,

00:34:46.969 --> 00:34:48.493
but actually
redeeming it in-store

00:34:48.493 --> 00:34:50.159
and causing the
in-store store behavior.

00:34:52.780 --> 00:34:55.982
And then the third area is
connecting online to in-store.

00:34:55.982 --> 00:34:57.690
So the way that we
did this when we first

00:34:57.690 --> 00:34:59.670
started working
with Food Lion, we

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:04.320
created a schema within their
dataset for Google Analytics

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:06.630
that whenever a
user logs in online,

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:10.740
we actually collected
that user's MVP loyalty

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:12.606
identification number.

00:35:12.606 --> 00:35:14.230
And we put that into
a custom dimension

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:15.272
into Google Analytics.

00:35:15.272 --> 00:35:17.730
And what that allowed was for
us to actually, every time we

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:21.270
needed to extract that data to
know what users were logged in

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:23.550
and were active on
Food Lion's website,

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:26.460
we were able to export that
out to Sheets for Food Lion's

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:28.490
business intelligence
team to work with,

00:35:28.490 --> 00:35:31.500
and to know what type of
data they were working with,

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:33.287
and what type of users they had.

00:35:33.287 --> 00:35:35.370
And so what Food Lion's
business intelligence team

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:37.161
was able to do in the
collaborative setting

00:35:37.161 --> 00:35:39.420
like Google Sheets is they
were able to actually take

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:43.560
all of those user IDs
to know who they were

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:46.094
and how many sessions
each of those users had.

00:35:46.094 --> 00:35:47.760
And then from there,
actually understand

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:50.450
what categories do
those users fall into.

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:51.960
And they could
compare and contrast

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:54.900
all of the data that was coming
in from their transaction

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:57.720
database on the back end
compared to their Google

00:35:57.720 --> 00:35:58.950
Analytics interface.

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:02.190
And so with that,
they were able to see

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:04.189
users that are existing
to the website,

00:36:04.189 --> 00:36:06.480
meaning they've actually been
logged in, and been using

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:07.380
the website.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:10.380
Users that were new to
the website that had just

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:11.910
started using it this month.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.770
As well as users who
were not actually

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:15.270
active on the website at all.

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.340
And immediately this
caused an impact inside

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:19.980
of Food Lion's
ecosystem of data to be

00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:22.270
able to say users who
were actually engaged

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:24.270
with the website and had
previously been engaged

00:36:24.270 --> 00:36:27.450
with the website are actually
spending more money in-store

00:36:27.450 --> 00:36:29.550
per month, which
is a huge benefit

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:32.850
to get insight into that because
as a website that doesn't

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:35.460
actually sell anything
what it relates

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:39.340
and what it connects to in-store
is actually what matters.

00:36:39.340 --> 00:36:41.480
So finally, to summarize,
the impact that we've

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:44.510
seen of combining Google
Sheets and Data Studio together

00:36:44.510 --> 00:36:47.420
has actually been just
simpler, faster reporting.

00:36:47.420 --> 00:36:49.190
We're able to get
insights much quicker.

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:52.610
We're able to move in a
much more agile fashion.

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:55.024
We have a faster, more
efficient reporting workflow.

00:36:55.024 --> 00:36:56.690
We've actually heard
back from Food Lion

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:58.400
that they're saving
about 25 hours

00:36:58.400 --> 00:36:59.870
every month that
they were spending

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:01.310
creating these reports.

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:03.230
They've reduced
that workflow time,

00:37:03.230 --> 00:37:06.170
so that actually helps their
internal process quite a bit.

00:37:06.170 --> 00:37:08.750
We've moved from multiple
platforms into one.

00:37:08.750 --> 00:37:12.710
We see this especially
with their team of social

00:37:12.710 --> 00:37:14.990
that we have logging
into Facebook Insights,

00:37:14.990 --> 00:37:17.240
logging into Twitter Analytics,
and being able to pull

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:18.550
that information down.

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:21.350
We've actually moved this into
Sheets and Data Studio for them

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:23.014
to get all that information.

00:37:23.014 --> 00:37:24.680
We moved from multiple
reports into one.

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:26.630
We're no longer sending
spreadsheets around.

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:29.900
We're no longer having
these different formats.

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:32.090
We have one multi-page
report inside

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:35.180
of Data Studio that actually
is giving their executive team

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:38.249
a high level view of everything
going on in their company.

00:37:38.249 --> 00:37:40.040
We have viewers that
can interact with data

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:41.870
like they couldn't before.

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:45.080
Previously, especially when we
talk about spreadsheet files,

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:47.404
you had to give the
raw dataset every time

00:37:47.404 --> 00:37:48.570
that you worked with people.

00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:50.278
But because of the
data governance inside

00:37:50.278 --> 00:37:52.040
of Data Studio, we
could have viewers

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:54.934
of that report that
actually could get insights

00:37:54.934 --> 00:37:57.350
from that report without needing
access to the data source

00:37:57.350 --> 00:38:00.840
itself, which was very
powerful for their situation.

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:03.197
And then finally, insights
can be found faster.

00:38:03.197 --> 00:38:05.030
At the end of the day,
if we're not actually

00:38:05.030 --> 00:38:08.300
finding insights in our data
and taking action on them

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:10.510
then we're kind of just
spinning our wheels.

00:38:10.510 --> 00:38:12.680
And so what we want to
see at the end of the day

00:38:12.680 --> 00:38:17.210
is for users to come in,
get insights, understand

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:19.760
what social posts are working,
understand what coupon product

00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:23.090
categories are actually
causing user behaviors that we

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:24.410
want to see in stores.

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:27.050
And so we're seeing that happen
with a combination of Google

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:28.674
Sheets and Data Studio.

00:38:28.674 --> 00:38:30.590
So next, I'll have Andy
from Whirlpool come up

00:38:30.590 --> 00:38:32.631
and talk about how they're
using these solutions.

00:38:38.164 --> 00:38:39.080
ANDY LEWIS: All right.

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:39.740
Hi folks.

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:41.450
Wow, that is loud.

00:38:41.450 --> 00:38:42.430
My name's Andy Lewis.

00:38:42.430 --> 00:38:44.150
I'm from Whirlpool corporation.

00:38:44.150 --> 00:38:46.920
I lead a team at Whirlpool
called the Winning Workplace.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:50.750
And what that team is is it
was a purpose built team.

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:53.240
In fact, it was purpose built
because we were the Google Go

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:55.430
Live team in the beginning.

00:38:55.430 --> 00:38:57.560
But it was purpose
built specifically

00:38:57.560 --> 00:39:00.440
to drive the adoption and
transformation of the Google

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:01.317
tools at Whirlpool.

00:39:01.317 --> 00:39:03.650
We wanted to make sure that
we were getting the most out

00:39:03.650 --> 00:39:05.774
of what we were doing, and
collaboration was really

00:39:05.774 --> 00:39:09.020
the most important part of this.

00:39:09.020 --> 00:39:12.140
The reason that we went with
Google in the first place

00:39:12.140 --> 00:39:14.210
was actually because we
wanted to drive a culture

00:39:14.210 --> 00:39:15.410
change at Whirlpool.

00:39:15.410 --> 00:39:16.970
Now, when I talk
about culture I'm

00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:22.076
really not talking
about our heritage.

00:39:22.076 --> 00:39:23.450
We've got a very
strong heritage.

00:39:23.450 --> 00:39:25.574
We're extremely proud of
our heritage at Whirlpool.

00:39:25.574 --> 00:39:27.350
We've been around
for 100 or so years.

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:30.080
Iconic brands like
KitchenAid and Maytag.

00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:33.170
What I'm really talking
about is our behaviors,

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:35.090
and how are we
collaborating with folks,

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:39.084
and how fast are we
to decision making.

00:39:39.084 --> 00:39:41.000
Who do we involve in the
different discussions

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:42.000
that we're having?

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:46.070
How siloed are how, and easy
is it to get to information?

00:39:46.070 --> 00:39:48.650
And so with that,
the only tool that we

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:51.740
felt that we could
accomplish this with

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:53.780
was just G Suite and Google.

00:39:53.780 --> 00:39:56.090
With that, we also
knew that it wasn't

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:57.939
going to be a quick
flip of the switch,

00:39:57.939 --> 00:40:00.230
and it was just going to
happen, that we needed to kind

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:01.320
of shepherd that through.

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:02.937
So a number of
things that we did.

00:40:02.937 --> 00:40:05.270
The first one was actually
we looked at our environment.

00:40:05.270 --> 00:40:08.780
And it was our physical
space, where we were sitting.

00:40:08.780 --> 00:40:11.030
Were we sitting next to the
folks we needed to sit to.

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:12.544
We have an open
office environment,

00:40:12.544 --> 00:40:14.460
and we felt that we could
do better with that.

00:40:14.460 --> 00:40:17.450
And so Winning Workplace started
out with our real estate team

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:20.170
first and foremost and actually
cleaned up that environment.

00:40:20.170 --> 00:40:21.890
We're refreshing all
of our buildings.

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:24.790
We have a beautiful new
North American headquarters,

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:27.260
and we got people into a
physical space that actually

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:29.360
helped with collaboration.

00:40:29.360 --> 00:40:34.430
On top of that, when we
looked at our virtual space,

00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:36.820
we've got employees
all over the globe.

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:40.160
100,000 employees in a
number of different regions.

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.130
And how could we
help enable them

00:40:43.130 --> 00:40:46.700
to collaborate across distances
the same way that we did

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:49.160
with the physical
environment and collaborating

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:51.230
within the buildings?

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:55.904
So with that, we now have G
Suite, and we put that in.

00:40:55.904 --> 00:40:58.070
We wanted to make sure that
we were actually getting

00:40:58.070 --> 00:41:00.600
collaboration appropriately.

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:03.680
So some of the
questions that we had,

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:06.440
and one in here at the
bottom is for today.

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:08.450
But some of the questions
we had was, first

00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:11.516
of all, what is
successful collaboration?

00:41:11.516 --> 00:41:14.140
Just because there's a number of
people that we're sharing with

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:15.260
and they're in the
different documents

00:41:15.260 --> 00:41:17.660
that doesn't mean that we're
actually collaborating.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:21.260
The second one is how are we
being effective as a team?

00:41:21.260 --> 00:41:22.610
And this is my team.

00:41:22.610 --> 00:41:25.167
Are we being effective
in the efforts

00:41:25.167 --> 00:41:26.750
that we're doing to
try and help folks

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:29.750
to collaborate and to get
better at this in G Suite

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:31.630
adoption and transformation?

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:33.380
And then the third one
today is why did we

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:35.254
use Data Studio to take
a look at all of this

00:41:35.254 --> 00:41:36.890
and put it all together.

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:41.464
So for us, collaboration, there
was a really easy indicator

00:41:41.464 --> 00:41:43.130
that we could look
at for collaboration,

00:41:43.130 --> 00:41:45.590
and it was in the Hangouts tool.

00:41:45.590 --> 00:41:47.322
Hangouts for us was
a real easy one,

00:41:47.322 --> 00:41:49.280
because it means every
time you've got somebody

00:41:49.280 --> 00:41:50.779
using a Hangout,
you've got somebody

00:41:50.779 --> 00:41:53.920
that's face-to-face that's not
sitting next to each other.

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:56.000
So that means that, yeah,
we are enabling folks

00:41:56.000 --> 00:41:58.130
to work across
distances appropriately.

00:41:58.130 --> 00:41:59.870
So we could even look
at this one-to-one

00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:01.520
and say, OK, every time
we're using Hangouts,

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:03.853
we're getting value out of
this, and we're helping folks

00:42:03.853 --> 00:42:06.620
to get their work done.

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:08.120
The things that we
looked at, if you

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:09.870
take a look at some
of the slides up here,

00:42:09.870 --> 00:42:11.240
we do look at our weekly usage.

00:42:11.240 --> 00:42:13.220
So how many Hangouts
are happening

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:14.780
on a day-to-day basis.

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:18.740
We also want to look at
not just that, but then

00:42:18.740 --> 00:42:21.000
how many actual meetings
are being done by Hangouts.

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:22.460
So not just the
individual people,

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:24.527
but the actual individual
meetings themselves

00:42:24.527 --> 00:42:26.360
that we know are being
conducted by Hangout.

00:42:26.360 --> 00:42:28.790
One of the interesting
metrics that we had

00:42:28.790 --> 00:42:31.830
was when we turned
Hangouts on in 2015

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:36.020
in January, after
a year, in 2016,

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:38.984
we were running at about
1,000 Hangouts per day.

00:42:38.984 --> 00:42:40.400
So that's not
individual Hangouts,

00:42:40.400 --> 00:42:42.170
but 1,000 meetings
were actually being

00:42:42.170 --> 00:42:44.360
held over Hangout per day.

00:42:44.360 --> 00:42:45.860
This year at that
time, we're now up

00:42:45.860 --> 00:42:48.590
to about 3,000 meetings
per day on Hangouts.

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:51.860
So just exponential
take off of this.

00:42:51.860 --> 00:42:54.620
But without being able to look
at the actual data behind it,

00:42:54.620 --> 00:42:56.250
we would have no idea.

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:58.092
So the trending is good.

00:42:58.092 --> 00:43:00.050
But the other thing that
we did was we invested

00:43:00.050 --> 00:43:01.850
in Chromebox for meetings.

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:03.470
And if you don't
know what those are,

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:05.990
those are the Hangout rooms,
so you can install them

00:43:05.990 --> 00:43:09.080
into a physical location,
tie them into your calendar,

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:10.770
and then walk in,
and with one click,

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:13.490
start a meeting up, and
get a Hangout going.

00:43:13.490 --> 00:43:15.650
Those have helped with
adoption tremendously,

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:18.630
because now it's very easy for
people in different locations,

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:20.570
especially in our
manufacturing plants,

00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:22.130
we've got those scattered
all over the globe,

00:43:22.130 --> 00:43:23.840
so we now can connect
to those places

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:27.200
like we never had
been able to before.

00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:29.120
The way that we put
this dashboard together,

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:32.570
and this is all in Data
Studio, is actually

00:43:32.570 --> 00:43:34.880
we pulled an APIs out of Google.

00:43:34.880 --> 00:43:38.130
And this is a unique solution
using both Data Studio

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:39.870
and Google Sheets.

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:42.510
So we pulled the APIs in
right into Google Sheets

00:43:42.510 --> 00:43:45.570
so that we could take a look at
all of that raw data in Sheets.

00:43:45.570 --> 00:43:47.550
Well, in that format,
it's not really that

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:50.170
easy to take a look at for us,
because there's so much of it,

00:43:50.170 --> 00:43:52.086
and it didn't answer the
questions immediately

00:43:52.086 --> 00:43:52.950
what we had.

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:55.710
So then what we did was we
pumped it all into Data Studio.

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:59.100
So now we've got these
really big robust dashboards,

00:43:59.100 --> 00:44:01.671
and then we can put those
URLs out and give those

00:44:01.671 --> 00:44:03.420
to our leadership and
they can take a look

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:06.840
and see how we're
performing with Hangouts.

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:08.790
The second one, though,
is that these Hangout

00:44:08.790 --> 00:44:11.280
rooms that we had put in,
these Chromebox for meetings,

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:13.377
the neat part about
this was that we just

00:44:13.377 --> 00:44:15.210
had a Google sheet in
there, and we manually

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:18.040
update that every time we add
another room to the inventory,

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:18.900
and then it pulls it back in.

00:44:18.900 --> 00:44:20.316
And now we've got
a dashboard that

00:44:20.316 --> 00:44:23.654
is coming through a couple
of different locations.

00:44:23.654 --> 00:44:26.070
But I have one place that I
can send leadership to and say

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:28.486
here's how we're adopting and
yes, we are getting the most

00:44:28.486 --> 00:44:29.514
out of our investment.

00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:35.060
So the second
question for us was

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:37.560
how are we with our personal--

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:40.100
the Winning Workplace,
training and embedment--

00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:43.880
and are we really helping folks
to get better at Google Tools?

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:45.966
So one of the things
that we do is we go out

00:44:45.966 --> 00:44:47.090
and we have these sessions.

00:44:47.090 --> 00:44:48.256
We call them immersion days.

00:44:48.256 --> 00:44:50.700
And we have folks
out there, we ask

00:44:50.700 --> 00:44:52.790
them to take three to four
hours out of their day,

00:44:52.790 --> 00:44:55.006
and we bring departments
on all at once.

00:44:55.006 --> 00:44:56.630
And then we work with
them to give them

00:44:56.630 --> 00:45:00.710
some help on just what's out.

00:45:00.710 --> 00:45:02.090
Sheets, Slides, Docs.

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:02.850
How to share.

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:04.520
How to use Drive better.

00:45:04.520 --> 00:45:07.041
The thing is it's a pretty big
investment for those folks,

00:45:07.041 --> 00:45:08.540
and it's an investment
for our team.

00:45:08.540 --> 00:45:10.310
And we want to make sure that
we're actually helping them

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:11.810
by going through all
of this training.

00:45:11.810 --> 00:45:13.790
Because you've all been
through training classes

00:45:13.790 --> 00:45:15.706
that you've sat through
the training and gone,

00:45:15.706 --> 00:45:18.080
well, there's three
hours of my life shot.

00:45:18.080 --> 00:45:20.730
So we're hoping to avoid that.

00:45:20.730 --> 00:45:23.589
So what we're looking at
here is a couple of things.

00:45:23.589 --> 00:45:25.130
One is we want to,
obviously, we want

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:28.160
to see the Google tools,
the usage increase.

00:45:28.160 --> 00:45:31.010
We also want to see the other
tools that we're using go away,

00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:33.770
some of the competitor tools
and other things in this space.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:35.880
So we're tracking both of those.

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:37.460
But the important
part of this is

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:40.190
that we're also
tracking the folks

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:42.200
and how they're feeling
about the tools.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:44.960
So we have surveys that
go out before each session

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:51.590
and ask people so what is your
self-proficiency in Slides,

00:45:51.590 --> 00:45:52.902
Sheets, Docs, Gmail.

00:45:52.902 --> 00:45:54.860
And then afterwards, we
ask them the same thing

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:58.460
and see if we've helped
them with their proficiency.

00:45:58.460 --> 00:46:03.530
So the way that we put
this together was we

00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:04.790
pull all of our Google data.

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:06.620
We actually put
this into BigQuery.

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:09.847
So it's a different dataset
than the Google Sheets.

00:46:09.847 --> 00:46:11.430
But we put this into
BigQuery and then

00:46:11.430 --> 00:46:13.850
we also put in a bunch of
data on the other tools

00:46:13.850 --> 00:46:16.190
that we're using as
well, and tie that all

00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:19.990
together into a few different
datasets and BigQuery.

00:46:19.990 --> 00:46:22.790
Then we actually put
in a hierarchy data

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:25.430
too, so we can actually
put in all of the people

00:46:25.430 --> 00:46:27.386
and understand
where they roll up

00:46:27.386 --> 00:46:28.760
in the organization
by department

00:46:28.760 --> 00:46:29.832
and by senior leader.

00:46:29.832 --> 00:46:31.790
And then we can take a
look at that information

00:46:31.790 --> 00:46:33.248
and understand,
OK, this department

00:46:33.248 --> 00:46:34.470
is doing really well.

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:38.072
And this department
needs some help.

00:46:38.072 --> 00:46:39.530
So that's the data
flow on that top

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:40.230
to get us to the dashboards.

00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:42.230
But on the bottom
one, what we've done

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:44.030
is we've used
Google Forms, and we

00:46:44.030 --> 00:46:46.490
send the self-assessment
questionnaire out

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:47.750
in a Google form.

00:46:47.750 --> 00:46:50.090
That information flows
into Google Sheets.

00:46:50.090 --> 00:46:55.310
We take some of that data, we
put it into the Data Studio

00:46:55.310 --> 00:46:56.270
to use for analysis.

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:58.370
But some of that we
leave right in Google

00:46:58.370 --> 00:47:01.150
Sheets, and we just
constantly update it.

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:02.900
And then now what that
does is it gives us

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:04.640
a little bit of a
tie-in and a context

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:08.120
to, OK, we just met
with this organization.

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:10.340
They didn't feel
really good beforehand.

00:47:10.340 --> 00:47:11.540
We did all the training.

00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:13.873
We're seeing the usage spike,
and now afterwards they're

00:47:13.873 --> 00:47:14.610
feeling great.

00:47:14.610 --> 00:47:16.250
That's what you're hoping for.

00:47:16.250 --> 00:47:19.580
What we actually have found out
by building this solution out

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:22.010
is we will go into
an organization,

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:23.262
say they need some help.

00:47:23.262 --> 00:47:24.470
They're not feeling too good.

00:47:24.470 --> 00:47:26.450
They're not very
confident in the tools.

00:47:26.450 --> 00:47:28.640
But boy, they're using it.

00:47:28.640 --> 00:47:31.730
So that means that the team
is out there actually using

00:47:31.730 --> 00:47:33.910
the tool for a lot of
their day-to-day work.

00:47:33.910 --> 00:47:35.660
But they still don't
feel competent at it.

00:47:35.660 --> 00:47:38.420
So either we need to help them
with a little bit of training

00:47:38.420 --> 00:47:40.020
or they're struggling.

00:47:40.020 --> 00:47:42.715
And it's just an
analysis we never

00:47:42.715 --> 00:47:44.090
would have gotten
to if we hadn't

00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:46.631
put all these things together
in the tools in the way that we

00:47:46.631 --> 00:47:47.930
did.

00:47:47.930 --> 00:47:50.770
So with that, I believe
I hand it off to Dan.

